  'body'        => <<<'HTML'
<p>If you are buying your first home, what to look for when buying a house comes down to five things: location, the true monthly cost, a layout that fits your life, the physical condition of the property, and how well it will hold its value. Get those right and most other details sort themselves out. This guide walks through each one the way I would with a friend who is about to tour their first house in Texas.</p>

<h2>1. Start with location, because you cannot remodel it</h2>
<p>You can replace countertops, paint walls and even add a room. You cannot move the house. Before you fall for a kitchen, make sure the neighborhood works for your daily life.</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Commute:</strong> Drive the route at rush hour, not on a Sunday afternoon. In Austin, Houston and Dallas, a few miles can mean a very different drive.</li>
<li><strong>Schools:</strong> Even without kids, school zoning affects resale. Check the district boundaries yourself rather than relying on a listing.</li>
<li><strong>Flood risk:</strong> In much of Texas this is non-negotiable homework. Look up the FEMA flood map, ask the seller for their disclosure, and ask your insurance agent for a quote before you commit.</li>
<li><strong>Daily needs:</strong> Groceries, parks, healthcare and the places you actually go on weekends.</li>
<li><strong>Future development:</strong> A vacant lot behind the house may not stay vacant. Check city or county planning sites for nearby projects.</li>
</ul>
<p>Our guide to <a href="/choosing-the-right-location-for-your-texas-dream-home/">choosing the right location for your Texas home</a> goes deeper on this, and most of it applies whether you build or buy.</p>

<h2>2. Know your real monthly cost, not just the price</h2>
<p>Beginners often focus on the list price and the mortgage payment. The number that matters is the full monthly cost of owning the home.</p>
<ul>
<li>Principal and interest on the loan</li>
<li>Property taxes, which are a big line item in Texas and vary widely by county, city, school district and special districts (MUDs)</li>
<li>Homeowners insurance, and flood insurance if needed</li>
<li>HOA dues, if any</li>
<li>Utilities, which can swing a lot with home size, age and insulation in our summers</li>
<li>A maintenance reserve, often estimated at around 1 percent of the home's value per year</li>
</ul>
<p>Many planners suggest keeping housing costs around 28 to 30 percent of gross income, but treat that as a ceiling, not a target. Get pre-approved so you know what a lender will offer, then decide what you are comfortable paying. A book like [[amz:books_first_time_buyer|a solid first-time homebuyer guide]] can help you walk through the math before you talk to a lender.</p>
<div class="callout"><p><strong>Pro tip:</strong> Ask the listing agent for the current tax bill and the last 12 months of utility costs. Then look up the tax rate yourself. A home that was homesteaded by a long-time owner may have a much lower bill than what you will pay after the sale.</p></div>

<h2>3. Check the size and layout against how you actually live</h2>
<p>Square footage alone tells you very little. Two homes of the same size can feel completely different depending on how the space is arranged.</p>
<h3>Questions to ask on every tour</h3>
<ul>
<li>Where would your bed, couch and dining table actually go? Bring a tape measure.</li>
<li>Is there a place for a home office if you work remotely even part of the time?</li>
<li>How far is the laundry from the bedrooms?</li>
<li>Is there enough storage, or will the garage become a storage unit?</li>
<li>Does the layout still work if your family grows or a parent moves in?</li>
</ul>
<p>A [[amz:laser_measure|compact laser measure]] makes it quick to grab room dimensions so you can compare homes later. If you are torn between layouts, our post on <a href="/how-to-choose-a-floor-plan-for-your-new-home/">how to choose a floor plan</a> covers the same thinking builders use.</p>

<h2>4. Look hard at condition before you get attached</h2>
<p>Cosmetic issues are cheap. Structural, water and system problems are not. During your showings, look for these warning signs:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Water:</strong> Stains on ceilings, soft spots in floors, musty smells, or fresh paint in just one spot.</li>
<li><strong>Foundation:</strong> Diagonal cracks above doors and windows, doors that stick, or sloping floors. Expansive clay soils in much of Texas make this worth watching.</li>
<li><strong>Roof:</strong> Ask its age. Curled or missing shingles and granules in the gutters are signs it may be near the end.</li>
<li><strong>HVAC and water heater:</strong> Check the data plates for the manufacture date. Older units may need replacing soon.</li>
<li><strong>Electrical:</strong> Two-prong outlets, flickering lights, or a panel that looks crowded or patched.</li>
<li><strong>Drainage:</strong> Soil that slopes toward the house or downspouts that dump next to the foundation.</li>
</ul>
<p>Then hire a licensed inspector. In Texas, inspectors are licensed by TREC, and you can usually negotiate repairs or credits during the option period. Bringing a few simple tools to your second showing helps you ask better questions.</p>
<!--products:outlet_tester,moisture_meter,headlamp|Simple tools for a smarter second showing-->
<p>If the home is new construction, do not skip the inspection just because it is new. Our <a href="/new-construction-inspection-checklist/">new construction inspection checklist</a> explains what to check and when.</p>

<h2>5. Think about resale and future growth</h2>
<p>Even if you plan to stay a long time, life changes. A house that will be easy to sell protects your biggest investment.</p>
<ul>
<li>Is the home roughly in line with others on the street, or is it the most expensive house in the neighborhood?</li>
<li>Are there features most buyers expect locally, such as a garage, a second bathroom or a usable yard?</li>
<li>Is the area seeing new jobs, roads and businesses, or is it losing them?</li>
<li>Does the HOA have reasonable rules and healthy reserves?</li>
</ul>
<p>Look at how long homes in the area tend to sit on the market and whether sale prices are close to list prices. Your agent can pull this from the MLS.</p>

<h2>6. Weigh buying against building</h2>
<p>Some buyers start shopping existing homes and realize nothing on the market fits. If that sounds familiar, building may be worth a look. It takes longer and requires more decisions, but you get modern systems, energy efficiency and a layout designed around you. Read <a href="/build-a-home-or-buy-an-already-built-one-weighing-the-pros-and-cons/">build or buy: weighing the pros and cons</a> before you decide.</p>

<h2>Next steps</h2>
<p>Write down your must-haves and deal-breakers before your next tour, and get pre-approved so you shop with a real budget. If you are leaning toward building instead, our <a href="/budgeting-for-your-texas-home-build-tips-for-staying-on-track/">Texas home build budgeting guide</a> is a good place to start, and the <a href="/free-training/">free training</a> walks you through the full process step by step.</p>
